#HELPS

0 PSIONICS~
Psionics are mental powers, given by remorting and now and then by the
immortals.  They come in four disciplines:

  Assault   ego whip, torment, nightmare, mindblast
  Astral    astral walk, shift, project, telekinesis
  Defense   mindbar, psionic armor, psychic shield, transfusion
  Control   clairvoyance, confuse, mind leech, enervate, pyrotechnics

The 2nd remort gives one power from each discipline, chosen at random, the
3rd a second from each and the 4th a third: 4, then 8, then 12.  They stack.
The 5th and final remort gives all 17 psionic skills.  A remort wipes your
skills, but the powers you were given come back each life at 1%, and a
remorted character holding fewer than their due is topped up at login.  A
power the immortals grant arrives with the next level you gain.

Each power opens at its own level, and below it you hold the power but can
neither use nor train it:

  17 psionic armor            21 confuse, enervate, nightmare, telekinesis
  18 clairvoyance, torment    22 mindbar, mind leech
  19 ego whip, project,       23 mindblast
     psychic shield           25 astral walk, shift
  20 pyrotechnics             28 transfusion

Every power starts at 1% and is practised with Salir in the Astral Plane
(WALKTO SALIR); he sells none of them.  Each one is its own command, and
a missed roll costs a little mana and no lag (pyrotechnics alone lags on a
miss).  Two take a short command word: ASTRAL <target> for astral walk,
EGO <target> for ego whip.

See also: REMORT, SALIR, PRACTICE
~

0 'ASTRAL WALK' ASTRAL~
Syntax: astral <character>

Carries you, and your pet if it stands with you, to another character
anywhere in the world.  Type ASTRAL, not ASTRAL WALK: the word after it is
the target, and ASTRAL WALK BOB looks for somebody called "walk".  2.BOB
picks the second Bob you can see.

The target must be somebody you can see, and never an immortal or a player
who has set NOSUMMON.  Their room must be one you may enter and travel
magic may reach: not no-recall, safe, private, solitary, jail, death trap,
gods-only, no-teleport, or a newbie room once you are past level 10; not
another class's guild hall or a room its guards would turn you from; and
not past one of Hyrule's gates you have not earned.  Your own room must not
be a jail.  Astral walk may take you out of a no-recall room, though never
into one.  It can be used in a fight.

Finding no path costs nothing.  Otherwise it costs 70 mana before the
roll, and against a mobile whose mind is immune to mental attack it fails,
or against a resistant one may fail, with the mana gone.  A missed roll
costs a little mana.

The crossing leaves you stunned until the next tick, so you arrive unable
to act: arriving beside something hostile is dangerous.

Level 25.  Like every psionic power it starts at 1% until practised with
Salir, and cannot be used below its level.  A missed roll costs a little
mana and no lag.

See also: PSIONICS, SHIFT, GATE, NOSUMMON
~

0 CLAIRVOYANCE~
Syntax: clairvoyance <character>

Shows you the room another character is standing in, anywhere in the
world, as LOOK would: its description, what lies there and who is there.
You do not move.  The target must be somebody you can see; immortals
cannot be scried, and a room that is private, a jail, a death trap,
gods-only, a newbie room or closed to teleports shows you nothing ("They
aren't here.").  A failed attempt shows only a fuzzy image.

It costs 25 mana and can be used in a fight.  Half the time the target
feels that somebody is watching them.

Level 18.  Like every psionic power it starts at 1% until practised with
Salir, and cannot be used below its level.  A missed roll costs a little
mana and no lag.

See also: PSIONICS, PROJECT, SCAN
~

0 CONFUSE~
Syntax: confuse [victim]

Throws a victim's mind into confusion; in a fight it defaults to your foe.
Each round a confused victim loses its turn wandering in a daze until it
shakes the confusion off -- a little over one round in ten -- and attacks
again.  It lasts four ticks, five from level 30 and six from level 40.

It costs your level plus 50 mana.  A mind immune to mental damage can never
be confused, and a mentally resistant one may throw it off; somebody already
confused is refused, and so is the treasure goblin, before anything is spent.

Level 21.  Like every psionic power it starts at 1% until practised with
Salir, and cannot be used below its level.  A missed roll costs a little
mana and no lag.

See also: PSIONICS, NIGHTMARE, EGO WHIP
~

0 'EGO WHIP' EGOWHIP~
Syntax: ego [victim]

Lashes a victim's ego for mental damage: half your level in four-sided
dice, plus half your level, halved by a saving throw.  Type EGO, not EGO
WHIP.  In a fight it defaults to your foe.

A victim who fails the save and is not already whipped also loses STR,
INT, WIS or DEX, chosen at random, for 4d5 ticks: 2 points, and one more
each at levels 15, 25 and 35.  It costs 20 mana.

Level 19.  Like every psionic power it starts at 1% until practised with
Salir, and cannot be used below its level.  A missed roll costs a little
mana and no lag.

See also: PSIONICS, TORMENT, MINDBLAST
~

0 MINDBAR~
Syntax: mindbar

Walls your own mind: mental damage and mental drains against you are
halved.  It works on you alone and takes no target.  It costs 50 mana,
lasts about a sixth of your level in ticks, and cannot be combined with
psionic armor or psychic shield.

Level 22.  Like every psionic power it starts at 1% until practised with
Salir, and cannot be used below its level.  A missed roll costs a little
mana and no lag.

See also: PSIONICS, PSIONIC ARMOR, PSYCHIC SHIELD
~

0 MINDBLAST 'MIND BLAST'~
Syntax: mindblast

Strikes the mind of everybody in the room you could attack -- not your
group, pets, charmed followers, shopkeepers or immortals -- for mental
damage that grows with your level to level 50, halved by a saving throw.
Everybody else in the area feels a wave of vertigo and takes nothing.  It
takes no target and costs 50 mana even when nobody is hit.

Level 23.  Like every psionic power it starts at 1% until practised with
Salir, and cannot be used below its level.  A missed roll costs a little
mana and no lag.

See also: PSIONICS, EGO WHIP, TORMENT
~

0 'MIND LEECH' MINDLEECH~
Syntax: mindleech <victim>

Mind Leech drains mental energy from a victim and restores half of the mana
actually taken to the user.  If the victim has no mana, the probe deals mental
damage instead.

A mind immune to mental attack gives up nothing at all, and a mentally
resistant mind may surrender only half.  Psionic defenses such as Mindbar
reduce whatever is left.  An ordinary mind cannot resist the drain.

Minimum level: 22

See also: PSIONICS, ENERVATE, MINDBAR
~

0 ENERVATE~
Syntax: enervate [victim]

Tears away a victim's movement and then their health, each from your level
to twice your level, and returns half of what it actually took to you.  A
mind immune to mental attack gives up nothing, a resistant one may lose
only half, and psionic defences such as mindbar reduce what is left;
damage that was resisted cannot become healing.  It costs 35 mana.  In a
fight it defaults to your foe.

Level 21.  Like every psionic power it starts at 1% until practised with
Salir, and cannot be used below its level.  A missed roll costs a little
mana and no lag.

See also: PSIONICS, MIND LEECH, TORMENT
~

0 NIGHTMARE~
Syntax: nightmare [victim]

Fills a victim's mind with horrors for a third of your level in ticks.
While it lasts they regain no hit points, mana or movement, lose some mana
at once, and a mobile's armour is worse.  A victim who saves is unharmed,
and the attempt still costs the mana and the wait and starts a fight;
somebody already in a nightmare is refused.  It costs 20 mana.  In a fight
it defaults to your foe.

Level 21.  Like every psionic power it starts at 1% until practised with
Salir, and cannot be used below its level.  A missed roll costs a little
mana and no lag.

See also: PSIONICS, CURE NIGHTMARE, CONFUSE
~

0 PROJECT~
Syntax: project <direction>

Sends your spirit out in a straight line in one of the ten directions while
your body stays where it is.  You need type nothing more: the spirit moves
on by itself, showing you each room it passes as LOOK would, for your level
divided by four, plus one, rooms, then returns to your body within the same
command.  It passes closed doors, and stops early at a missing, secret or
wizlocked exit or a room it may not enter.

Others may see the glow rise out of you, and a ghostly figure passing
through their room.  It costs 25 mana.

Level 19.  Like every psionic power it starts at 1% until practised with
Salir, and cannot be used below its level.  A missed roll costs a little
mana and no lag.

See also: PSIONICS, CLAIRVOYANCE, SCAN
~

0 'PSIONIC ARMOR' PSIONIC~
Syntax: psionic [character]

Armours a mind against mental attack, cutting mental damage by a quarter
for half your level in ticks.  Type PSIONIC alone for yourself, or PSIONIC
<name> for somebody in the room: PSIONIC ARMOR looks for a character
called "armor".  It costs 20 mana and cannot be combined with mindbar or
psychic shield.

Level 17.  Like every psionic power it starts at 1% until practised with
Salir, and cannot be used below its level.  A missed roll costs a little
mana and no lag.

See also: PSIONICS, MINDBAR, PSYCHIC SHIELD
~

0 'PSYCHIC SHIELD' PSYCHIC~
Syntax: psychic

Shields every member of your group in the room who has no mental defence
yet, cutting mental damage by a quarter for half your level in ticks.  It
takes no target, must be used standing, and costs 50 mana.  It does not
replace mindbar, psionic armor or another psychic shield.

Level 19.  Like every psionic power it starts at 1% until practised with
Salir, and cannot be used below its level.  A missed roll costs a little
mana and no lag.

See also: PSIONICS, PSIONIC ARMOR, MINDBAR
~

0 PYROTECHNICS~
Syntax: pyrotechnics <victim>

This psionic skill ignites the air around a victim.  It deals the same
damage as a fireball cast at your level, halved by a saving throw, and may
heat a piece of the victim's equipment until it temporarily cannot be worn -
which is what it offers over a fireball, and what its extra mana pays for.
A failed attempt costs its wait, just as a failed cast does.  It does not
consume a light source.

Minimum level: 20

See also: PSIONICS, FIRE SPELLS, HARMFUL
~

0 SHIFT~
Syntax: shift <character>

Pulls another character to you through a spatial rift.  It does not move
you: the target arrives in your room.

It is refused, with nothing spent, when the target is you, an immortal or
already beside you; when either of you is fighting; for an aggressive
mobile, a mobile immune to summoning or a player with NOSUMMON; when your
room is no-recall, a teleport, river or arena room, or one you may not
use magic from; and when the target stands in a safe, no-recall, newbie or
otherwise protected room, or one travel magic may not reach.

Otherwise it costs 70 mana.  A mobile may resist the pull, with the mana
and the wait spent.  Opening the rift leaves you stunned until the next
tick, so it cannot be used to pull a victim in and strike first.

Level 25.  Like every psionic power it starts at 1% until practised with
Salir, and cannot be used below its level.  A missed roll costs a little
mana and no lag.

See also: PSIONICS, ASTRAL WALK, SUMMON, NOSUMMON
~

0 TELEKINESIS TK~
Syntax: telekinesis <object name>
Syntax: tk <object name>

Telekinesis allows the user to translocate a visible, takeable ground item
from elsewhere in the world.  It respects carry limits, ownership, quest-item
binding, and protected or no-teleport rooms.  Money, treasure, hidden
containers, carried items, and NOLOCATE items cannot be retrieved this way.
It takes the first item by that name it finds, which need not be yours.

It looks before it reaches.  If nothing by that name lies anywhere it can
reach, or the only match is an item telekinesis can never fetch, you are
told so and it costs nothing.  Otherwise your skill decides whether you
hold concentration: a miss costs a little mana, a success the full 50.
Like every psionic power it starts at 1% until practised; practise it
with Salir.

A corpse can be fetched whole, gear and all, even when the gear inside it
is NOLOCATE.  Once a corpse rots, NOLOCATE gear that fell out of it has to
be fetched on foot.

Minimum level: 21

See also: PSIONICS, DISARM, STEAL
~

0 TORMENT~
Syntax: torment [victim]

Makes a victim relive old pain: mental damage from half your level to
three times your level, halved by a saving throw.  It costs 20 mana.  In a
fight it defaults to your foe.

Level 18.  Like every psionic power it starts at 1% until practised with
Salir, and cannot be used below its level.  A missed roll costs a little
mana and no lag.

See also: PSIONICS, EGO WHIP, MINDBLAST
~

0 TRANSFUSION~
Syntax: transfusion <character>

Gives 50 of your own hit points to another character in the room, up to
their maximum.  You lose exactly 50 whatever they gain.  It costs 20 mana,
and cannot be used when doing so would reduce you to zero hit points: you
need more than 50.  It refuses yourself and anyone already at full health.
It can be used in a fight, and on anybody in the
room -- it does not ask whose side they are on.

Level 28.  Like every psionic power it starts at 1% until practised with
Salir, and cannot be used below its level.  A missed roll costs a little
mana and no lag.

See also: PSIONICS, AID, HEAL
~

-1 EXOTIC WEAPONS WEAPON WEAPONSMASTER AXE DAGGER FLAIL MACE POLEARM SPEAR SWORD
 WHIP~
Each weapon skill applies to a specific group of armaments, and determines how
well a character fights with a particular weapon.  The skills provide
talent in all weapons (except exotics), from chair legs to halberds.
If the character has the parry skill as well, he can parry incoming attacks.
The chance of parrying is best if the character in question is skilled at
both his weapon and his opponent's.

The weapon skills consist of the following:

axe             the use of axes, ranging from hand to great (but not halberds)
dagger          the use of knives and daggers, and other stabbing weapons
flail           skill in ball-and-chain type weapons
mace            this skill includes clubs and hammers as well as maces
polearm         the use of pole weapons (except spears), including halberds
spear           this skill covers both spears and staves, but not polearms
sword           the warrior's standby, from rapier to claymore
whip            the use of whips, chains, and bullwhips
exotic          the use of strange magical weapons
bow             the use of weapons that can shoot to another room

The exotic skill cannot be purchased, and is dependent solely upon level.

Note that all characters may use all weapons, but that the minimum level
they need to be for a given class of weapons and the starting percentage of
skill at each weapon type is dependent on their primary class.

Trainer: Rakar (all)

See also helps on ARCHERY and PARRY.
~

-1 AGGROSTAB~

This skill gives you a chance of attacking aggressive monsters before they
attack you. Thieves get the benefit of having a chance to do backstab
damage, provided they are learned in backstab. This skill is available only
to members of the thieves' guild.  Only players with a Thief background get
the backstab benefit attached to this skill.

The minimum level to use this skill depends on your primary class and is:

Mage:30  Cleric:30  Thief:15  Warrior:24  Monk:N/A  Necromancer:N/A

The number of trains needed to gain this skill depends on your primary
class and is:

Mage:7  Cleric:7  Thief:4  Warrior:6

Trainer: Quickfingers (?/T)

Monks and necromancers cannot learn aggrostab, since they cannot join the
thieves' guild.

See also: BACKSTAB, HIDE, STEALTH
~

0 BACKSTAB BS~
Syntax: backstab <victim>
        bs <victim>

Opens a fight with a blow from behind: your round's first two hits (three
if you are hasted) land with the backstab's multiplier, which grows with
your level and is greater with a dagger than any other weapon.  A victim
who is not awake takes double and is always hit.

You need a weapon wielded, any kind.  You cannot backstab while you are
fighting anybody, and a victim hurt below a third of their health is too
suspicious to approach ("hurt and suspicious") -- that refusal still costs
the six seconds the stab would.  A practised backstabber sometimes lands a
FATALITY instead.  Hit or miss, it costs six seconds of lag and starts the
fight.

Usable from level: Mage 20, Cleric 24, Thief 3, Warrior 14; never Monk,
Necromancer.  Trainers: Ni Hi the ninja master; Tia and the Mud School adept
practise it only.  GAINLIST shows who sells it to your class and guild.

See also: FATALITY, HIDE, SNEAK, STEALTH
~

0 BASH~
Syntax: bash [victim]

Throws your whole weight into a foe to knock them down; with no name it
bashes the foe you are fighting.  The chance is your skill, raised by the
weight you carry and lowered by theirs (gear and coin count), by being the
smaller of the two, by their dexterity against your strength, and by
level: ten points a level of difference.  Riders and steeds cannot be
bashed, nor somebody already down.

A bash that lands deals a little damage and costs the victim two rounds of
lag -- it delays what they type and a mobile's special attacks, not their
ordinary blows -- and you six seconds.  A bash that misses puts you on the
ground, resting, with nine seconds of lag, and resting you take more damage
until you are struck back to your feet.

Usable from level: Mage 20, Cleric 5, Thief 15, Warrior 3, Monk 12; never
Necromancer.  Dwarves are born holding bash at 1%, whatever their class.
Trainers: Geko the gladiator, the Master of Movement; Jazair practises it
only.  GAINLIST shows who sells it to your class and guild.

See also: TRIP, KICK, SHOVE, DIRT
~

0 BERSERK~
Syntax: berserk

A battle rage: you heal twice your level in hit points and gain hit and
damage of a fifth of your level, but your armour class worsens by ten
points for every five levels, and every skill you use is weaker by half
your level while it lasts.  It lasts about an eighth of your level in
ticks; it is not ended by the end of a fight.

It needs 50 mana and costs 50, and halves your movement.  A failed attempt
costs 25 mana, half your movement and nine seconds of lag; a success three
seconds.  It is easier when you are hurt or already fighting.  It is
refused while you are calmed, berserk or frenzied.

Usable from level: Mage 30, Cleric 29, Thief 35, Warrior 18; never Monk,
Necromancer.  Trainer: Geko the gladiator, to members of the warrior guild.
GAINLIST shows who sells it to your class and guild.

See also: FRENZY, ENHANCED DAMAGE, BASH
~

0 'BLINDING FISTS'~
Syntax: blind <victim>

This is an assortment of various punches aimed at a foe, delivering
multiple blows. Only monks of level 20 or above have the quick reflexes
and amazing agility to use this martial art.

Number of trains: 3
Trainer: Master of Movement (Monk)

See also: FISTS OF FURY, CRANE DANCE, IRON SKIN
~

0 'CRANE DANCE' CRANE~
Syntax: crane

The adept martial artist has many specialized movements in his repertoire
of hand-to-hand combat. The crane dance is one of the most lethal,
launching the adept into the air towards his opponents, striking
everything in a series of lightning-quick jabs.

It is an opening: it cannot be started once a fight is under way.  Each
foe it strikes loses about a quarter of its health if it is weak, a
smaller share the stronger it is, and never more than 25 hit points for
each of your levels.  It costs 30 mana, and half your movement when it
lands (a quarter when it misses everyone).

This skill is exclusively for the use of monks of level 35 and up.

Number of trains: 5
Trainer: Master of Movement (Monk)

See also: BLINDING FISTS, FISTS OF FURY, IRON SKIN

~

0 'DANGER SENSE'~
Syntax: danger

This skill tells you where the other players around the area are and how far
away from you, in very general terms.  It also reveals how many stealthed
players are in the area.  It is available only to members of the warrior
guild.

The minimum level to use this skill depends on your primary class and is:

Mage:36  Cleric:36  Thief:31  Warrior:13  Monk:N/A  Necromancer:N/A

The number of trains needed to gain this skill depends on your primary
class and is:

Mage:1  Cleric:1  Thief:2  Warrior:2

Monks and necromancers may not gain this skill, since they may not join
the warrior guild.

Trainer: Breark (?/W)

See also: DODGE, PARRY, FAST HEALING
~

0 DESPAIR~
Despair is a passive combat skill available at level 25.  When a character
with this skill tries to FLEE, there is a chance that instead the opponent
is overcome by a wave of hopelessness and flees the battle, leaving the
character where they stand.  The chance is the skill's percentage.

This skill cannot be activated manually; it fires when you flee.

Trainer: Rakar the weaponsmaster, in Dresden (any class, any guild).

Number of trains: 1  (available to all classes at level 25)

See also: BERSERK, FATALITY, SECOND ATTACK
~

-1 DESTRUCTION~
This skill is gainable only by full warriors (W/W). Gaining this skill
gives the user the chance to destroy his or her opponent's weapon.  The
chances are very slim, but they still exist.  It may not be used before
level 25.

Number of trains needed: 4
Trainer: Vladamir (W/W)

See also: BASH, SMITE, DISARM
~

0 DIRT 'DIRT KICKING' DIRT-KICK~
Syntax: dirt [victim]

Kicks dirt in a foe's eyes; with no name, the foe you are fighting.  A kick
that lands deals a little damage and blinds them, -4 to hit, until the next
tick.  Your dexterity against twice theirs, speed and level decide it, and
the ground matters: easier in a desert or field, harder indoors, in a city
or on a mountain, and impossible on water or in the air ("There isn't any
dirt to kick").  Somebody already blind is refused.  Hit or miss, six
seconds of lag.

Usable from level: Mage 15, Cleric 15, Thief 6, Warrior 8, Monk 20; never
Necromancer.  Trainers: Geko the gladiator, Ni Hi the ninja master, the
Silent Master.  GAINLIST shows who sells it to your class and guild.

See also: BLINDNESS, BASH, TRIP
~

0 DISARM~
Syntax: disarm

Tries to strike the weapon from the hand of the foe you are fighting; it
takes no name.  You need a weapon of your own, or hand to hand.  Your
skill with your weapon and with theirs, your dexterity against their
strength, and level decide it.  Hit or miss, six seconds of lag.

A disarmed weapon falls to the floor, where a mobile that sees it may pick
it up again; one that cannot be dropped goes into its owner's pack.  A
weapon under BEWITCH WEAPON will not budge, and a warrior of the warriors'
guild may hold on with HEROIC GRIP.

Usable from level: Mage 18, Cleric 17, Thief 16, Warrior 12, Monk 16; never
Necromancer.  Trainers: Dolonar the blacksmith, the Master of Movement; the
dwarven blacksmith practises it only.  GAINLIST shows who sells it to your
class and guild.

See also: HEROIC GRIP, BEWITCH WEAPON, BASH
~

-1 DODGE~
In the words of one wise warrior, 'the best way to block a blow is to not
be where it lands'.  The dodge skill honors this tradition, by improving the
character's natural agility to the point where many blows will miss the
target. The chance of dodging is also affected by the dexterity of the
attacker and the target.

The minimum level to use this skill depends on your primary class and is:

Mage:19  Cleric:22  Thief:5  Warrior:13  Monk:5  Necromancer:23

The number of trains needed to gain this skill depends on your primary
class and is:

Mage:5  Cleric:5  Thief:3  Warrior:4  Monk:3  Necro:5

Trainer: Geko (?/W), Dolonar (?/W), Suma (?/T), Qualo (?/C), Diemos (?/M),
          mud school adept (practice only, no gain), Silent Master (monk)
          Necromantic Knight (necro),

See also: PARRY, SHIELD BLOCK
~

-1 'ENHANCED DAMAGE'~
Monks and members of the warriors guild can become skilled enough in
combat that they are able to inflict more damage than other classes.  Enhanced
damage is checked for with each hit, although with a low skill, the chance
of receiving a bonus is very low indeed.

The minimum level to use this skill depends on your primary class and is:

Mage:31  Cleric:31  Thief:30  Warrior:16  Monk:14  Necromancer:N/A

The number of trains needed to gain this skill depends on your primary
class and is:

Mage:7  Cleric:7  Thief:6  Warrior:3  Monk:5

Necromancers may not learn enhanced damage.

Trainer:  Dolonar (?/W), Silent Master (monk)

See also: SECOND ATTACK, THIRD ATTACK, FATALITY
~

-1 FATALITY~
This skill gives the user a chance to kill his or her victim in a single
blow.  It is not a command but, rather, each backstab carries a small chance
of causing a fatality.  Only pure thieves (T/T) of level 26 or above
have the knowledge of anatomy and the dexterity to cause fatality.

The chance, in percent, for each backstab: one for every 20% you know the
skill (5 at 100%), one more for dexterity above 22 and another above 24,
and three more against a monster at least ten levels below you.  It is
halved against a player and doubled against a sleeping victim.  At 100%
that is 5 to 10 percent, or up to 20 against a sleeping monster.

There is no chance at all against a victim of a higher level than yours,
and none against a few creatures that cannot be finished that way: the
training dummy, the treasure goblin, Ganon and Gohma.  A victim already
below a third of its health cannot be backstabbed in the first place.

Number of trains: 5
Trainer: Mizry (T/T)

See also: BACKSTAB, DESTRUCTION
~

0 'FISTS OF FURY' FISTS~
Syntax: fists <victim>

Fists of Fury is a more aggressive version of Blinding Fists, focused on
using strengthened blows as well as increased speed.  It requires the
mental discipline that is found only in monks of level 30 and above.

Number of trains: 4
Trainer: Master of Movement (Monk)

See also: BLINDING FISTS, CRANE DANCE, STUNNING BLOW
~

-1 'HAND TO HAND'~
Learning the rare hand to hand style of fighting gives the player a weapon
even when disarmed -- bare hands.  Trained hand to hand experts are far more
effective than many swordsmen, and those of the monk class are even more
effective with it.

The minimum level to use this skill depends on your primary class and is:

Thief:15  Warrior:6  Monk:1

Number of trains required depends on your primary class and is:

Thief:4  Warrior:4 Monk:free

Magic using classes have notoriously delicate hands, preventing them from
learning hand to hand combat.

Trainer: Breark (W/W, T/W), Silent Master (monk), mud school adept (practice
         only, no gain)

See also: DUAL WIELD, SECOND ATTACK, BASH
~

0 'IRON SKIN' IRON~
Syntax: iron

Through a lifetime of meditation and study, monks possess the unique
ability to alter the texture of their skin for short periods of time. The
skin retains the flexibility of normal skin, but is as hard as the coldest
steel.  Monks must reach level 22 in order to use iron skin.

Three times in four it also makes you immune to one kind of weapon --
blunt, piercing or slashing, chosen when your skin hardens. You are told
which, and AFFECTS shows it for as long as it lasts.

Number of trains: 2
Trainer: Master of Movement (monk)

See also: BLINDING FISTS, FISTS OF FURY, CRANE DANCE
~

0 KICK~
Syntax: kick

Kicks the foe you are fighting for one to your level in damage; it takes
no name and needs a fight.  Hit or miss, three seconds of lag.

Usable from level: Mage 16, Cleric 12, Thief 14, Warrior 4, Monk 3; never
Necromancer.  Trainers: Breark the troll, the Silent Master; the dwarven
blacksmith and the Mud School adept practise it only.  GAINLIST shows who
sells it to your class and guild.

See also: BASH, TRIP, DIRT
~

0 LEVITATE~
Syntax: levitate

A monk's float: you are flying for a tenth of your level in ticks.  It
always works, costs 10 mana and no lag, and is refused if you are already
airborne or have under 10 mana.  Flying carries you over water and through
the air like the FLY spell.

Usable from level: Monk 13; never Mage, Cleric, Thief, Warrior, Necromancer.
Trainer: the Master of Movement.  GAINLIST shows who sells it to your class
and guild.

See also: FLY, PSIONICS
~

0 'NERVE DAMAGE'~
Syntax: nerve <victim>

Intimate knowledge of anatomy has enabled the studious monks to
concentrate on certain nerve centers in the bodies of their opponents when
forced into battle.  Striking these vulnerable areas causes great pain and
damage.  Monks receive this skill for free but must study until level 15
before they can use it.

Trainer: Silent Master (Monk)

See also: STUNNING BLOW, BLINDING FISTS
~

-1 PARRY~
If at first you fail to dodge, block it.  Parry is useful for deflecting
attacks, and is successful more often than dodge.  Parry requires a weapon for
full success.  The hand-to-hand skill may also be used, but results in reduced
damage instead of no damage.  The best chance of parrying occurs when the
defender is skilled in both his and his opponent's weapon type.

The minimum level to use this skill depends on your primary class and is:

Mage:22  Cleric:20  Thief:13  Warrior:8  Monk:9  Necromancer:N/A

Number of trains required depends on your primary class and is:

Mage:6 Cleric:6  Thief:5  Warrior:free  Monk:3

Necromancers cannot learn to parry.

Trainer: Dolonar (?/W), Stout(?/T), Kalak (W/?), Silent Master (Monk),
         mud school adept (practice only, no gain)

See also: DODGE, SHIELD BLOCK
~

0 RESCUE~
Syntax: rescue <character>

Steps between a member of your group and whatever is fighting them, so it
fights you instead.  The one you rescue must be a player in your group who
is fighting; pets and other mobiles do not need your help, and somebody you
are already fighting is "too late".  Your skill alone decides it.  Hit or
miss, three seconds of lag.

Usable from level: Mage 26, Cleric 20, Thief 18, Warrior 7; never Monk,
Necromancer.  Trainers: Dolonar the blacksmith; the dwarven blacksmith and
the Mud School adept practise it only.  GAINLIST shows who sells it to your
class and guild.

See also: GROUP, FLEE, BASH
~

-1 'SLEIGHT OF HAND'~
Those learned in the arts of thievery, have such heightened reflexes and
senses that they can pick up items or get items from objects in broad
daylight unbeknownst to the acute observer.

The minimum level to use this skill depends on your primary class and is:

Mage:10  Cleric:18  Thief:6  Warrior:15  Monk:20  Necromancer:N/A

Number of trains required depends on your primary class and is:

Mage:3  Cleric:3  Thief:1  Warrior:3

Monks and necromancers cannot learn this skill, as they cannot join the
thief guild.

Trainer: Suma (?/T)

See also: STEAL, PEEK, PICK LOCK
~
0 STEAL~
Syntax: steal <object> <character>
        steal platinum|gold|silver|copper <character>

Takes something from another's pack, or a tenth or less of one pile of
their coin.  Worn things cannot be stolen, nor anything that cannot be
dropped or is above your level, nor anything you could not carry.  The
victim must not be fighting; a sleeping one is far easier.  Your chance is
capped at 85%, and anybody more than five levels above you is always too
wary.  Hit or miss, six seconds of lag.

Fail and the victim cries out: a mobile attacks you, and stealing from a
player makes you WANTED.  You cannot steal while in STEALTH.

Usable from level: Mage 11, Cleric 11, Thief 8, Warrior 10; never Monk,
Necromancer.  Thieves get it free.  Trainers: Quickfingers the pickpocket;
Tia and the Mud School adept practise it only.  GAINLIST shows who sells it
to your class and guild.

See also: PEEK, WANTED, HIDE
~

0 STEALTH~
Syntax: stealth

A full thief's art: nobody without holylight or the Triforce can see you
at all -- not in the room, not by DETECT HIDDEN, not by WHERE -- for 12
ticks.  A success halves your current movement points; a failure costs
nothing.  Metal armour cuts the chance to a third.

It is refused while you are sneaking or hiding, and for two ticks after a
fight with another player.  Striking anybody ends it, and so does VISIBLE.
You cannot STEAL while in stealth.

Usable from level: Thief 18; never Mage, Cleric, Warrior, Monk, Necromancer.
Trainer: Mizry the assassin, to thieves of the thieves' guild.  GAINLIST
shows who sells it to your class and guild.

See also: HIDE, SNEAK, SHADOWMELD, VISIBLE
~

0 'STEEL FIST' STEEL STEELFIST~
Syntax: steel

By altering their body's chemistry, monks have the option of increasing the
density of molecules surrounding their hands. This has the effect of
increasing their damage roll and may be used starting at level 25.

Number of trains: 3
Trainer: Silent Master (Monk)

See also: BLINDING FISTS, FISTS OF FURY, IRON SKIN
~

0 'STUNNING BLOW'~
Syntax: stun <victim>

This skill allows the martial artist to strike at an exposed part of his
or her opponent, stunning the victim for a brief period of time.  The
accuracy required by this skill limits its use to monks of level 21 or
above.

Number of trains: 3
Trainer: Silent Master (Monk)

See also: NERVE DAMAGE, BASH, KICK
~

0 TRIP~
Syntax: trip [victim]

Takes a foe's feet from under them; with no name, the foe you are
fighting.  Being smaller than them, their dexterity, their speed and level
count against you, and every attempt has a little bad luck in it.  Flying
victims, riders and steeds, and somebody already down cannot be tripped.

A trip that lands deals a little damage and costs the victim a round of
lag (their typed commands and a mobile's special attacks, not their
ordinary blows) and you six seconds; a miss costs you four.  Tripping
yourself puts you flat on your face for twelve.

Usable from level: Mage 21, Cleric 19, Thief 1, Warrior 15, Monk 10; never
Necromancer.  The thieves' guild and monks get it free.  Trainers: Ni Hi the
ninja master; the Silent Master and the Mud School adept practise it only.
GAINLIST shows who sells it to your class and guild.

See also: BASH, KICK, DIRT
~

-1 'SECOND ATTACK'~
Training in second attack allows the character a chance at additional strikes
in combat. A 100% second attack does NOT guarantee 2 attacks every
round.

The minimum level to use this skill depends on your primary class and is:

Mage:24  Cleric:22  Thief:12  Warrior:5  Monk:8  Necromancer:N/A

Number of trains required depends on your primary class and is:

Mage:5 Cleric:4  Thief:3  Warrior:2  Monk:3

Members of the warrior guild receive this skill for free.

Necromancers cannot learn second attack.

Trainer: Breark (?/W), Ni Hi (?/T), Dominic (C/C), Fredar (?/C),
         Kilar (?/M), Master of Movement (Monk), Flame the fire
         Mage (M/M)

See also: THIRD ATTACK, DUAL WIELD
~

0 'FOURTH ATTACK'~
There is no fourth attack.  Third attack is the last of the extra strikes a
character can train, and haste, dual wield and your weapon's own speed are
where the rest of a round's blows come from.

See also: THIRD ATTACK, DUAL WIELD, HASTE
~

-1 'THIRD ATTACK'~
Training in third attack allows the character a chance at an additional strike
in a combat, and increases the chance of a second attack as well.  Perfect
third attack does NOT assure three attacks per round.  Only full warriors
(W/W) may gain third attack and they may use it at level 15 and above.

Number of trains: 4
Trainer: Vladamir (W/W)

See also: SECOND ATTACK, DUAL WIELD
~

0 TRACK~
Syntax: track <character>

Tells you which way to go to reach somebody: "You find a trail <direction>
from here", and whether they are just beyond (a room), not far off (five
rooms or fewer), some way off (fifteen or fewer) or a long way off.  It
does not move you; follow it and track again.  A missed roll says the
trail was lost and never points a wrong way.

The target must be somebody you can see, anywhere in the world.  The trail
follows the six ordinary directions only -- not diagonals, portals or
other ways through -- and does not cross the sky or deep water unless you
are flying.  Each attempt costs 3 movement points, path or no path, and a
moment's lag; with 2 or fewer you are too tired to track.

Usable from level: Thief 25; never Mage, Cleric, Warrior, Monk, Necromancer.
Trainer: Mizry the assassin.  GAINLIST shows who sells it to your class and
guild.

See also: SCAN, WHERE, DANGER SENSE
~

-1 'FAST HEALING'~
The fast healing skill improves wound healing rates, whether walking, resting,
or sleeping. It represents knowledge of healing herbs or just general
toughness and stamina.  Fast healing is checked every tick, and it is
possible for it to fail.

The minimum level to use this skill depends on your primary class and is:

Mage:15  Thief:16  Warrior:11  Monk:18

Number of trains required depends on your primary class and is:

Mage:6  Thief:4  Warrior:3  Monk:4

Necromancers and Clerics cannot learn fast healing.

Trainer: Breark (?/W), Silent Master (Monk)

See also: MEDITATION
~

-1 HAGGLE HAGGLING~

Haggling is an indispensable skill to the trader.  It allows a character to
match wits with a merchant, seeking to get a better price for merchandise,
or to buy at the lowest possible cost.  Unfortunately, most merchants are
already very skilled at haggling, so the untrained adventurer had best
guard his treasure closely.

The minimum level to use this skill depends on your primary class and is:

Mage:7  Cleric:18  Thief:1  Warrior:14  Monk:N/A  Necromancer:N/A

Number of trains required depends on your primary class and is:

Mage:5  Cleric:5  Thief:3  Warrior:5

Monks and Necromancers cannot learn haggle, since they cannot join the
thief guild.

Trainer: Suma (?/T)

See also: SLEIGHT OF HAND, SKILLS
~

0 HIDE~
Syntax: hide

Slips you into the shadows of the room, out of the room's list for anybody
without DETECT HIDDEN, holylight or the Triforce.  It takes no time and
costs nothing, and tells you whether it worked.  You cannot hide in a
fight, a fighter can always be seen, and any command you type brings you
out again: hiding is for waiting, not for walking.  For moving unseen see
SNEAK and STEALTH.

Usable from level: Mage 10, Cleric 16, Thief 2, Warrior 12; never Monk,
Necromancer.  Hobbits are born holding it; thieves get it free.  Trainers:
Warder the nightmaster; Tia and Fingers practise it only.  GAINLIST shows
who sells it to your class and guild.

See also: SNEAK, STEALTH, DETECT HIDDEN, BACKSTAB
~

0 SNEAK~
Syntax: sneak

Moves you quietly: while it lasts, rooms you enter and leave are not told.
It takes no time, costs nothing and does not say whether it worked -- a
success lasts your level in ticks.  Any metal armour you wear cuts the
chance to a third ("that noisy armor"), and putting metal on while sneaking
rolls it again at that third.  It cannot be used while you are in STEALTH.

Usable from level: Mage 8, Cleric 12, Thief 4, Warrior 10, Necromancer 7;
never Monk.  Elves are born holding it; thieves get it free.  Trainers:
Warder the nightmaster; Tia, Fingers and the Mud School adept practise it
only.  GAINLIST shows who sells it to your class and guild.

See also: HIDE, STEALTH, BACKSTAB
~

0 'LISTEN AT DOOR'~
Syntax: listen <direction>

This skill is used at a door to determine if there are any mobs on the
other side of the door.  It is only available to members of the thieves'
guild.

The minimum level to use this skill depends on your primary class and is:

Mage:35  Cleric:35  Thief:17  Warrior:27  Monk:N/A  Necromancer:N/A

The number of trains needed to gain this skill depends on your primary
class and is:

Mage:1  Cleric:1  Thief:1  Warrior:1

Monks and necromancers may not gain this skill, as they cannot join the
thief guild.

Trainer: Warder (?/T)

See also: PEEK, SEARCH, STEALTH
~

0 LORE~
Syntax: lore <object>

Researches something you carry: its type, weight and worth, its armour or
weapon dice, its affects, and for potions, scrolls, pills, wands and
staves the spells in it, each named only as often as your skill.  Every
number is an estimate, anything from half to double the truth.  It takes
nine seconds, and costs gold: twenty times the object's level, less twice
your skill and half your level, possibly to zero for simple items.  An
object that cannot be identified says only that it is magical.

Usable from level: Mage 5, Cleric 6, Thief 12, Warrior 20, Monk 25; never
Necromancer.  Trainer: Stout the fence.  GAINLIST shows who sells it to your
class and guild.

See also: IDENTIFY, COMPARE, LOCATE OBJECT
~

-1 MEDITATION~
This skill is similar to fast healing, but relies on concentration and
mantras to increase mana recovery when the character is sleeping or resting.
Having the skill is all that is required to make it work; you will
automatically try to meditate when you are healing.

The minimum level to use this skill depends on your primary class and is:

Mage:6  Cleric:6  Thief:15  Warrior:15  Monk:9  Necromancer:8

Number of trains required depends on your primary class and is:

Mage:4  Cleric:4  Thief:5  Warrior:6  Monk:free  Necro:4

Trainer: Silius (?/C), Diemos (?/M), Necromantic Knight (necro),
         Master of Movement (Monk)

See also: FAST HEALING
~

-1 PEEK~
The peek skill is useful for seeing what a player or monster is carrying,
the better to use the steal command with.  More intelligent characters are
harder to peek at.  Peek is used automatically when looking at a character.
This skill is for members of the thieves' guild only.

The minimum level to use this skill depends on your primary class and is:

Mage:21  Cleric:21  Thief:10  Warrior:14  Monk:N/A  Necromancer:N/A

Number of trains required depends on your primary class and is:

Mage:5  Cleric:5  Thief:3  Warrior:5

Monks and necromancers cannot learn to peek, since they cannot join the
thieves' guild.

Trainer: Quickfingers (?/T)

See also: STEAL, HIDE SNEAK, SEARCH
~

0 PHASE~
Phase is a passive skill available at level 25.  When a character with
the phase skill goes to SLEEP, there is a chance they phase out of sight
as they drift off, as though they had used STEALTH.  The chance is the
skill's percentage.

This skill triggers automatically when you sleep; no command is required.

Number of trains: 1  (available to all classes at level 25)

See also: STEALTH, SNEAK, HIDE
~

0 PICK 'PICK LOCK'~
Syntax: pick <container>
        pick <direction>

Picks a lock without its key.  It costs three seconds before anything
else, so every refusal costs that too.  You cannot pick in a fight, and an
awake mobile more than five levels above you standing in the room is too
close to the lock.

Some locks refuse before any roll and say why: a lock beyond any pick, a
magical lock, one with no key, or something not closed or not locked.  A
fumble -- the pick slipping, the tumblers dropping back -- is a missed roll
on a lock that can open, and is worth trying again.  A trapped lock goes
off when you get it right, and stays locked; SEARCH first.

Usable from level: Mage 23, Cleric 23, Thief 7, Warrior 25; never Monk,
Necromancer.  Thieves get it free.  Trainers: Picky the locksmith; Tia and
the Mud School adept practise it only.  GAINLIST shows who sells it to your
class and guild.

See also: SEARCH, UNLOCK, DOORBASH, KNOCK
~

0 RIDE RIDING MOUNT DISMOUNT~
Syntax: ride <creature>
        mount <creature>
        dismount

Rides a mountable creature, which then pays the movement points.  You need
a saddle, carried or already on the creature, which goes on it as you
mount.  The creature must be free: not fighting, not anybody's follower,
not ridden; and you may not keep a pet and a mount at once.  A failed
attempt throws you for 25 hit points; your chance is capped at 90%.

A mount cannot go indoors, into a law room, through a closed or secret
exit, or into another class's guild; the sky needs one that flies, deep
water one that flies or swims.  Riders and steeds cannot be bashed or
tripped.  DISMOUNT gets down.

Usable from level: Mage 6, Cleric 6, Thief 5, Warrior 4, Monk 5, Necromancer
6.  Trainer: Seraloi the lost.  GAINLIST shows who sells it to your class
and guild.

See also: PET, FLY, MOVEMENT
~

0 SEARCH~
Syntax: search <container>
        search <direction>

Looks for a trap on a closed, locked container or door, and disarms one
it finds.  It costs six seconds whatever happens, refusals included.  An
awake mobile more than five levels above you in the room notices you
snooping and stops you.

A successful search on a trapped lock removes the trap (a door's from both
sides) and says so; on an untrapped one it says there is none.  A missed
roll says only that it might be trapped, whether or not it is -- and half
the time a missed roll on a trapped lock sets the trap off.  Traps burn,
gas, poison, blind, stun, teleport, summon a guardian or worse.

Something that is not a container, not closed, not locked, or cannot be
trapped is refused.

Usable from level: Mage 9, Cleric 11, Thief 7, Warrior 11; never Monk,
Necromancer.  Trainers: Quickfingers the pickpocket; Tia and the Mud School
adept practise it only.  Thieves get it free.  GAINLIST shows who sells it
to your class and guild.

See also: PICK, TRAPS, PEEK
~

0 SMITE~
Syntax: smite <victim>

A warrior's crushing round: every hit in it carries extra damage from the
weapon's own dice, scaled by your skill -- once, twice above level 30 and
three times above level 50.  A sword does it best; an axe, flail or mace a
little less; any other weapon gets no bonus.  In a fight it always strikes
the foe you are fighting.  You need a weapon wielded.  Hit or miss, six
seconds of lag.

The force strains your weapon: now and then a smite wears it down, and you
are told when it groans.  A weapon worn to nothing breaks and falls into
your pack -- keep it repaired and it never will.

Usable from level: Warrior 10; never Mage, Cleric, Thief, Monk, Necromancer.
Trainer: Vladamir the veteran, to warriors of the warriors' guild.  GAINLIST
shows who sells it to your class and guild.

See also: BASH, REPAIR, DESTRUCTION
~


-1 WANDS STAVES SCROLLS~
Magical items require training to use properly.  If your character lacks the
necessary skill to use an item, he or she will fail, possibly destroying it.  The
item skills are as follows:

scrolls         The reading of magical scrolls and books (see 'help recite')
staves          The use of staves and similar devices (see 'help brandish')
wands           The use of wands and similar items (see 'help zap')

The minimum level to use these skills depends on your primary class.
GAINLIST shows whether your class and guild can buy them.

Minimum levels for the use of scrolls are:

Mage:3  Cleric:4  Thief:5  Warrior:9  Monk:15  Necromancer:3

Number of trains required depends on your primary class and is:

Mage:free  Cleric:free  Thief:3  Warrior:3  Necromancer:free

Members of the mage and cleric guilds receive this skill for free.

Trainer: David (C/?), Gioli (M/?), Undead Spirit (necro), Dawn (?/M),
         mud school adept (practice only, no gain)


Minimum levels for the use of staves are:

Mage:5  Cleric:2  Thief:9  Warrior:9  Monk:34  Necromancer:7

Number of trains required depends on your primary class and is:

Mage: 2 Cleric:free  Thief:3  Warrior:3  Necromancer:2

Members of the cleric guild receive this skill for free.

Trainer: Silius (?/C), Dawn (?/M - practice only, no gain), Diemos (?/M),
         David (C/?), Necromantic Knight (necro), mud school adept
         (practice only, no gain)


Minimum levels for the use of wands are:

Mage:2  Cleric:5  Thief:9  Warrior:9  Monk:33  Necromancer:6

Number of trains required depends on your primary class and is:

Mage:free  Cleric:2  Thief:3  Warrior:3  Necromancer:free

Members of the mage guild receive this skill for free.

Trainer: Dawn (?/M - practice only, no gain), Diemos (?/M), Gioli (M/?),
         Silius (?/C), Soul Trapper (necro), mud school adept (practice
         only, no gain)

See also: LORE, BREW, CONCOCT
~

-1 'SHIELD BLOCK' SHIELDBLOCK BLOCK~

Shield block is a rather fancy name for the art of parrying with a shield.
Characters with no shield block skill will not be able to defend themselves
well with a shield.  Beware, flails ignore shield blocking attempts, and
whips have an easier time getting around them.  Axes may split shields in two.

The minimum level to use this skill depends on your primary class and is:

Mage:17  Cleric:17  Thief:16  Warrior:2  Monk:15  Necromancer:20

The number of trains needed to gain this skill depends on your primary
class and is:

Mage:5  Cleric:3  Thief:5  Warrior:free

Trainer: Kalak (W/?), Geko (?/W), mud school adept (practice only, no gain)

See also: PARRY, DODGE
~


0 $~

#$
